---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import QuoteForm from "../../components/QuoteForm.astro";
import FAQ from "../../components/FAQ.astro";
import CTA from "../../components/CTA.astro";
import { serviceSchema } from "../../lib/schema";
import { SITE_URL, PRICE, SUBURBS, SERVICES, GUARANTEE_FULL } from "../../config";

const service = SERVICES.find((s) => s.slug === "rental-pool-compliance")!;
const url = `${SITE_URL}/services/${service.slug}/`;
const linkedSuburbs = [
  SUBURBS.find((s) => s.slug === "cranbourne")!,
  SUBURBS.find((s) => s.slug === "narre-warren")!,
  SUBURBS.find((s) => s.slug === "point-cook")!,
  SUBURBS.find((s) => s.slug === "tarneit")!,
  SUBURBS.find((s) => s.slug === "werribee")!,
  SUBURBS.find((s) => s.slug === "frankston")!,
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Who is responsible for pool compliance on a rental property — landlord or tenant?",
    answer:
      "The property owner. Registration, certification, and the four-year re-inspection cycle are all the owner's legal obligation, regardless of who's living in the property or what the lease says.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I require the tenant to cover the cost of the inspection?",
    answer:
      "Generally no — the compliance obligation itself sits with the owner and can't be shifted to the tenant through a lease clause. Lease terms can reasonably ask tenants to keep the barrier area clear and report issues, but the underlying responsibility remains with you.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need to give the tenant notice before an inspection?",
    answer:
      "Yes — the same standard entry notice requirements that apply to any property access apply here. We can work with your property manager to arrange appropriate timing.",
  },
  {
    question: "What if a tenant has placed furniture or plants near the fence that causes a fail?",
    answer:
      "It's a common and easily fixed issue — usually just relocating the item away from the non-climbable zone. As the owner, arranging that fix is your responsibility, though it's often a quick conversation with the tenant or property manager.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do you offer bulk booking across a rental portfolio?",
    answer:
      "Yes — get in touch and we can help coordinate scheduling across multiple properties, with each still charged at our standard flat fee per property.",
  },
];
---
<Layout
  title={service.metaTitle}
  description={service.metaDescription}
  path={`/services/${service.slug}/`}
  schema={[serviceSchema(service, url)]}
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: "Services", path: "/services/" }, { name: service.name, path: `/services/${service.slug}/` }]} />

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-navy-900 mb-4 max-w-3xl">{service.h1}</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-navy-600 max-w-2xl mb-8">{service.summary}</p>

    <div class="prose-content max-w-3xl">
      <p>
        Landlords and property managers carry exactly the same pool and spa barrier compliance obligations as
        owner-occupiers — there's no lighter-touch version of the rules for investment properties. Registration,
        the Form 23 Certificate of Barrier Compliance, and the four-year re-inspection cycle all apply, and
        responsibility for all of it sits with the property owner rather than the tenant, throughout the tenancy.
      </p>

      <h2>Why rental properties need particular attention</h2>
      <p>
        A few patterns come up more often in tenanted properties than in owner-occupied homes. Tenants sometimes add
        garden features, pot plants or outdoor furniture without realising they need to stay clear of the barrier's
        non-climbable zone. Gate hardware issues that an owner-occupier might notice and fix immediately can go
        unreported. And for landlords managing several properties, each on its own four-year certification clock, a
        missed renewal on one property is easy to lose track of among routine leasing and maintenance tasks.
      </p>

      <h2>What the inspection covers</h2>
      <p>
        The assessment is the same thorough check as any standard inspection: fence and gate height (minimum
        1200mm), gaps under and within the fence, the non-climbable zone around the barrier, and gate self-closing
        and self-latching function. We coordinate directly with property managers where needed to arrange access and
        tenant notice.
      </p>
    </div>

    <CTA
      heading={`Keep your rental compliant — ${PRICE} flat`}
      subtext="We track your four-year renewal automatically so nothing slips between lease cycles."
    />

    <div class="prose-content max-w-3xl">
      <h2>Building compliance into routine property management</h2>
      <p>
        A few practical habits make ongoing compliance considerably easier across a rental property or portfolio:
        record your Form 23 issue date in the property file and set a reminder well ahead of the four-year expiry;
        include a basic visual barrier check as part of routine property inspections; and confirm compliance status
        at every change of tenancy, which is a natural checkpoint for catching anything that's changed. Read our full
        breakdown in the <a href="/guides/landlord-pool-compliance-vic/">landlord compliance guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The cost of letting it lapse</h2>
      <p>
        Non-compliance penalties can exceed $1,600, and there's no reduced enforcement for rental properties —
        councils follow up on investment properties the same as any other. A compliance issue discovered partway
        through a tenancy, or right before a sale, is more disruptive to resolve under time pressure than one caught
        as part of routine, proactive management.
      </p>

      <h2>Pricing</h2>
      <p>
        Rental pool compliance inspections are charged at the same {PRICE} flat fee as any standard inspection.
        {GUARANTEE_FULL} See our <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a> for full details, and get in touch about
        coordinating multiple properties across a portfolio.
      </p>

      <h2>Related guides</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/guides/landlord-pool-compliance-vic/">Landlord pool compliance in Victoria</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/four-year-reinspection-cycle/">The four-year re-inspection cycle explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/register-pool-with-council/">How to register a pool with council</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Available across Melbourne</h2>
      <p>We inspect rental properties across the metro area, including:</p>
      <ul class="columns-2 sm:columns-3">
        {linkedSuburbs.map((s) => (
          <li><a href={`/locations/${s.slug}/`}>Rental pool compliance {s.name}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p><a href="/locations/">See all service areas &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>

    <div id="quote-form" class="max-w-3xl mx-auto my-12">
      <QuoteForm />
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <FAQ faqs={faqs} heading="Frequently asked questions about rental pool compliance" />
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
